{"kind":"report","text":"VERDICT: approved, at exact clean pushed head 46c7665bcd71c761044dbe4bc0fb6386cc35ee01 on request/identity-lookup-actor-at, against artifact de8950ef, answering codex request 804f578c under my promise f9d2a736.\n\nI filed changes-requested on the earlier identity head because three instant-selection mutations survived, two failing open in revocation and expiry. That finding is now closed, and I closed it by killing the mutations myself rather than by reading a report that said they were killed.\n\nMUTATIONS I RAN, each applied alone to host/identity/resolve.go and reverted before the next. Baseline green first.\n\nSigner-fallback, replacing r.lookup(actor, record.at) with r.lookup(record.actor, record.at): kills four tests including TestLookupActorAtUsesTheNamedActorAndExactRecordInstant, the one that claims it.\nUnbounded position, substituting position 1\u003c\u003c30 at the record's own timestamp: kills TestLookupActorAtUsesAnchorPositionAtSameTimestamp and TestLookupActorAtUsesRevocationPositionAtSameTimestamp.\nPosition plus one: kills the same two position tests.\nPosition minus one: kills the same two.\nZero timestamp, keeping the record's position: kills TestLookupActorAtUsesInclusiveNotAfterTimestamp.\n\nAll four named mutations die, and each dies naming its own reason. resolve.go hashed identical before and after the whole sequence and the worktree was clean afterwards. One further mutation I attempted — ignoring the record entirely — does not compile, because `record` goes unused. That is a compiler-enforced floor under the guard rather than a coverage gap, and I record it as such rather than as a failed experiment.\n\nPRODUCTION IS UNCHANGED, AND I CHECKED THE CLAIM RATHER THAN TAKING IT. My first comparison was against main, which shows host/identity/resolve.go modified — that is the LookupActorAt function itself, added by the earlier commit f42bcc2e. Against f42bcc2e, the head I previously rejected, only host/identity/resolve_test.go changed and resolve.go is byte-identical. Codex's claim is accurate and correctly scoped; my first reading of it was not, and I am recording that so nobody re-derives a contradiction that is not there.\n\nTHE TESTS ASSERT DESIRED BEHAVIOUR, NOT TRANSCRIBED BEHAVIOUR. Each carries a positive and a negative side across the boundary rather than a single observed value. The signer-independence test is the strongest of them: it anchors the record signer as a genuinely different identity, asserts LookupAt returns that signer, and asserts LookupActorAt returns the named actor from the same record. A stub could not pass both halves. The two position tests each place a bystander record at the same whole second immediately before the anchor or revocation, which is exactly the shared-timestamp case where an off-by-one silently returns a revoked or unanchored actor.\n\nSECURITY. Every boundary fails closed in the dangerous direction. Unknown actor resolves empty; unknown record resolves empty; at a revocation's own position the actor is unanchored; after NotAfter the actor is unanchored. NotAfter is inclusive at its own value, which is the specified semantic and the safe reading is the one implemented. The property that matters most here is that the named actor is independent of the record's signer — without it, an actor could resolve as whoever signed a record they chose — and that is now pinned by a test that would fail if the two were conflated. I found no fail-open path.\n\nARCHITECTURE, and I want a ratifier to be able to disagree with me here. The affected layer is host/identity, the application-host public surface. docs/reference/architecture.md line 814 names \"the exported surface of host and host/identity\" as a Surface-layer contract, and this head's branch does add an exported method to that surface. The page is not updated in this head. I judge that it does not need to be: the host/identity row at line 837 describes capabilities rather than enumerating methods, it already covers \"two-axis resolution with a plain display at an exact verified record position\", and the pre-existing LookupAt is not named there either. Requiring a page edit for LookupActorAt while LookupAt goes unmentioned would make the row a changelog and would be inconsistent. No mechanical gate enumerates identity symbols, so nothing contradicts the page today. A ratifier who reads line 814 more strictly than I do would reach changes-requested instead, and that reading is defensible; I am stating my reasoning rather than hiding the question.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I found no test fluff removable without weakening a condition. The four tests are close to minimal: each is one boundary with both sides. TestLookupActorAtUsesTheNamedActorAndExactRecordInstant also covers unknown-actor and unknown-record, which could be split out, but combining them costs nothing and splitting would add fixture duplication. I recommend no cuts.\n\nGATES I RAN MYSELF AT THIS EXACT HEAD, in a dedicated detached worktree: gofmt -l silent, go build ./... clean, go vet ./... clean, git diff --check against main clean, and go test -count=1 ./... exit 0 with all 30 packages ok and nothing skipped.\n\nONE THING THE REQUESTER AND A MERGER SHOULD KNOW, which is not a defect in this head. Earlier today, gating a different head on this machine, a single full-suite run failed in cmd/gitseq-mcp and a second run at the identical tree passed. I recorded that as assert 9d2097f3 and I am not calling it flaky. My run here was clean, but one clean full-suite run on this machine is weaker evidence than it reads as, and that applies to this verdict as much as to anyone else's report.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:de8950ef403b7322233ebb0fd9b116de9167fade","head":"46c7665bcd71c761044dbe4bc0fb6386cc35ee01","verdict":"approved"}}